fsync on OpenBSD: table of threads waiting on several futexes (no
futex_waitv), shared by ntdll and wineserver; see the comment in the file.

Index: include/wine/fsync_openbsd.h
--- include/wine/fsync_openbsd.h.orig
+++ include/wine/fsync_openbsd.h
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+/*
+ * fsync on OpenBSD: waiters on several futexes
+ *
+ * OpenBSD has futex(2), and shared futexes work across processes on the
+ * shm_open() mapping that fsync uses, but there is no futex_waitv.  A thread
+ * that waits on several futexes (a multi-object or alertable wait) registers
+ * in a table in the fsync shared memory with a mask of the buckets of the
+ * futexes it waits on, and sleeps on its own futex word.  A wake scans the
+ * table and wakes the waiters whose mask has the bucket of the futex.
+ *
+ * Layout of the reserved shm slots (never allocated to objects):
+ *   slot 0: int[0] generation, int[1] waiters on it (used when the table is
+ *           full), int[2] number of registered waiters
+ *   slots 1 .. FSYNC_WAITERS: struct fsync_waiter
+ *
+ * The bucket of a futex is its shm index modulo 64: slots are 16 bytes and
+ * the shm pages are mapped page-aligned, so bits 4-9 of the address are the
+ * same in every process.
